var BANK_PARTS = BANK_PARTS || [];
BANK_PARTS.push([
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Unrepresentative sample","d":1,"s":"A poll of people who wrote letters opposing a new harbor ordinance found that most thought the ordinance was confusing. The sponsor concludes that most city residents find the ordinance confusing.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["infers that the ordinance caused the confusion merely because the letters mention both","treats people who wrote opposing letters as though they stood for city residents generally","dismisses the letters because the writers might gain if the ordinance fails","slides from one meaning of \"confusing\" in the letters to another meaning in the conclusion","treats two proposed wordings of the ordinance as the only possible wordings"],"a":1,"e":"The letters come from opponents, and the conclusion is about residents at large. That group is being asked to stand in for a wider one. The attractive choice — infers that the ordinance caused the confusion merely because the letters mention both — loses because the conclusion is about whose opinions were counted, not about the ordinance producing confusion. A causal story is a different criticism from the narrow sample."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"False dichotomy","d":1,"s":"The hospital board argues: either visiting hours are cut in half, or patients will get no rest. Visiting hours will not be cut in half. Therefore, patients will get no rest.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["infers that the whole hospital lacks rest because one ward lacks rest","dismisses a nurse's objection because of the nurse's schedule preferences in this instance","treats last month's restful nights as proof about next month","slides between a medical sense of \"rest\" and a legal sense of \"rest\"","treats a cut in visiting hours and a total loss of rest as the only two possibilities"],"a":4,"e":"From the refusal to cut hours, the board infers no rest at all. That follows only if those are the only options, and nothing has shown that they are. The attractive choice — infers that the whole hospital lacks rest because one ward lacks rest — loses because the argument does not move from a ward to the hospital. It pretends that cutting hours and losing all rest exhaust the ways rest might be protected."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Appeal to authority","d":1,"s":"A celebrated novelist says the city should repeal its noise ordinance. Therefore, the city should repeal the noise ordinance.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["rejects the ordinance because of the motives of the council that passed it","infers repeal from a correlation between novel sales and noise complaints","treats a novelist's endorsement as settling a legal question outside the novelist's demonstrated field","generalizes from one block's noise to every block in the city, though the conclusion is citywide only in the novelist's mouth","treats quieter nights and repealed rules as the only two options"],"a":2,"e":"A legal repeal is inferred from a literary celebrity's remark, with no showing that the remark applies a relevant standard. The attractive choice — rejects the ordinance because of the motives of the council that passed it — loses because nobody's motive is attacked. The entire reason given is the novelist's say-so."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Absence of evidence","d":1,"s":"No complaint log at the clinic mentions a missed allergy check. The director concludes that no allergy check has been missed.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["treats the lack of logged complaints as proof that no check was missed","takes for granted that \"missed\" in the logs means something other than \"missed\" in the conclusion","infers that the whole clinic missed checks because one department did","dismisses the nurses' concern because of the nurses' motives","draws the conclusion from a sample of patients who already had allergies"],"a":0,"e":"A log that never mentions a miss is being asked to prove there was no miss. Silence is not that proof unless a miss would have been logged. The attractive choice — takes for granted that \"missed\" in the logs means something other than \"missed\" in the conclusion — loses because the argument does not switch senses of a word. It promotes silence in the log into a clean bill of health."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Correlation vs. causation","d":1,"s":"Districts that adopted evening court hours saw civil filings rise in the same year. A commentator concludes that the evening hours caused the rise in filings.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["takes for granted that civil filings cannot rise in a district that keeps only daytime hours","rejects the commentator because bar groups might gain from evening hours","treats the filings of districts that adopted the hours as if the conclusion were about districts that did not in this instance","concludes that the evening hours produced the rise solely because the hours and the rise occurred in the same year","uses \"filing\" for a criminal charge in the premise and a civil complaint in the conclusion"],"a":3,"e":"Same-year movement is the only link, and it is asked to prove that the hours caused the filings. The commentator does not invoke, or depend on, a rule that filings had to rise first. The attractive choice — takes for granted that civil filings cannot rise in a district that keeps only daytime hours — loses because the commentator does not say filings are impossible without evening hours. The commentator says the evening hours caused the rise in the districts that adopted them. A necessary-condition claim about every district is more than the argument uses."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Equivocation","d":3,"s":"The baker asked for a \"light\" loaf, meaning a loaf that is easy to carry. The lab sheet uses \"light\" for a loaf with little sugar. This loaf is light on the lab sheet, so a clerk says it is what the baker asked for.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["infers that low sugar caused the loaf to be easy to carry from the mere use of one word","appeals to the lab as an authority on what bakers want to carry","treats two recipes as the only recipes the baker will accept","slides from \"light\" as easy to carry to \"light\" as low in sugar","takes for granted that the result could not occur at all unless the factor behind the claim that the baker asked for a \"light\" loaf, meaning a loaf that is easy to carry was present"],"a":3,"e":"The credited description is the mistake this argument makes. The attractive choice — infers that low sugar caused the loaf to be easy to carry from the mere use of one word — loses because it names a mistake arguments often make, using this argument's subject, but it is not the step this argument takes."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Circular reasoning","d":3,"s":"The mileage log is correct because it matches the driver's memory, and the driver's memory is correct because it matches the mileage log.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["infers that the driver's memory caused the log entries merely because the two agree","concludes that every company log is correct from this one log","treats the driver as an authority on odometers because the driver is well known","reads a drop in the percentage of checked trips as a drop in their number","offers each of two claims as the sole support for the other"],"a":4,"e":"Memory is vouched for by the log, and the log is vouched for by memory. Nothing outside the pair is offered. The attractive choice — infers that the driver's memory caused the log entries merely because the two agree — loses because the argument is not a causal claim about how the entries were produced. It is a closed loop of support."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Past-to-future","d":2,"s":"This kiln has reached the glaze temperature on every firing this month. The potter concludes that it will reach that temperature on the next firing.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["infers that the glaze temperature caused the pots to sell, because sales and firings are both part of the shop in this instance","treats this kiln as representative of every kiln in the guild","extends an unbroken record one firing forward without showing that the next firing matches the ones already run","appeals to a famous potter's praise of the kiln","lets the kiln and the pyrometer certify each other"],"a":2,"e":"A month of hits supports the next firing only if the next firing is like those hits. The argument supplies the streak and skips the comparison. The attractive choice — infers that the glaze temperature caused the pots to sell, because sales and firings are both part of the shop in this instance — loses because sales are not in the argument. The conclusion is about the next firing's temperature, not about a cause of sales."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Weak analogy","d":2,"s":"A quiet-car rule reduced complaints on the commuter rail. The transit chief concludes that the same rule will reduce complaints on the overnight ferry.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["transfers the rail result to the ferry without a likeness that matters to the complaints","infers that the quiet-car rule caused the rail drop merely from the rule and the drop being mentioned together, and the conclusion is only about the ferry","treats each rail car as quiet and therefore the whole train as quiet, which is not the conclusion","appeals to a celebrity rider's endorsement of quiet cars","treats the lack of ferry studies as proof the rule will fail, which is not what the chief concludes"],"a":0,"e":"Complaints fell under a rail rule, and that drop is handed to an overnight ferry. No shared cause of the complaints is supplied. The attractive choice — infers that the quiet-car rule caused the rail drop merely from the rule and the drop being mentioned together, and the conclusion is only about the ferry — loses because even if the rail premise were thinly causal, the argument's new step is the move to the ferry. The choice attacks a premise the argument treats as given and misses the analogy."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Ignoring alternatives","d":2,"s":"The cellar floor was wet at dawn. The keeper concludes that the sump pump was left off overnight.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["infers that the pump caused the wet floor from a controlled comparison of two cellars","treats one possible cause of a wet floor as though it were the only available cause","treats the lack of a maintenance log as proof the pump is new","gives the whole building a leak because the cellar is wet","takes for granted that the result could not occur at all unless the factor behind the claim that the sump pump was left off overnight was present"],"a":1,"e":"The only link offered is that the two things moved together, and the argument treats that as causation. The attractive choice — infers that the pump caused the wet floor from a controlled comparison of two cellars — loses because it names a mistake arguments often make, using this argument's subject, but it is not the step this argument takes."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Unrepresentative sample","d":2,"s":"Volunteers who maintain the east creek path mostly said the path needs more gravel. The office concludes that most people who use the path want more gravel.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that most people who use the path want more gravel follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case.","A path ought to receive more gravel only if most of its users request gravel.","Most users live within a mile of the creek.","With respect to wanting more gravel, the maintainers' views match the views of path users.","Most cases outside this one also fit the claim that most people who use the path want more gravel, whether or not they share its circumstances."],"a":3,"e":"The credited choice is the one that does the job this question asks. The attractive choice — Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that most people who use the path want more gravel follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case. — loses because it would carry the conclusion if it were granted, but the argument does not need a claim that strong. Sufficient is not the same as necessary."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Absence of evidence","d":2,"s":"No inspector has found a split in the red kayak. The outfitter concludes that the red kayak has no split.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that the red kayak has no split follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case.","The outfitter has never missed a split in any kayak the inspectors did not inspect.","A kayak can have at most one split.","Most cases outside this one also fit the claim that the red kayak has no split, whether or not they share its circumstances.","If the red kayak had a split, an inspector would have found it."],"a":4,"e":"The credited choice is the one that does the job this question asks. The attractive choice — Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that the red kayak has no split follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case. — loses because it would carry the conclusion if it were granted, but the argument does not need a claim that strong. Sufficient is not the same as necessary."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Weak analogy","d":2,"s":"A clipboard rule reduced lost badges at the craft museum. The director concludes that the same rule will reduce lost badges at the science hall.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["Any rule that reduces losses at a small museum will reduce losses at every larger museum.","The science hall has more visitors per day.","Badges are lost for the same kind of reason, in a comparable handoff, at both museums.","The craft museum began the rule during a holiday week.","The science hall already uses numbered lockers for coats."],"a":2,"e":"Without a relevant likeness, the craft-museum result does not reach the science hall. The conclusion depends on that likeness. The attractive choice — Any rule that reduces losses at a small museum will reduce losses at every larger museum. — loses because the argument is about this clipboard and these two sites, not about every rule and every larger museum. That broader transfer can fail and this one can still be supported by a likeness in how badges go missing."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Sufficient/necessary confusion","d":2,"s":"Anyone who finishes the harbor radio course receives a yellow tag. Quinn has a yellow tag. Therefore, Quinn finished the harbor radio course.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["The only way to receive a yellow tag is to finish the harbor radio course.","Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that Quinn finished the harbor radio course follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case.","Everyone who finishes the harbor radio course receives a yellow tag.","Most people who finish the course clip the tag to a collar.","Most cases outside this one also fit the claim that Quinn finished the harbor radio course, whether or not they share its circumstances."],"a":0,"e":"The credited choice is the one that does the job this question asks. The attractive choice — Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that Quinn finished the harbor radio course follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case. — loses because it would carry the conclusion if it were granted, but the argument does not need a claim that strong. Sufficient is not the same as necessary."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Percentages vs. numbers","d":3,"s":"At the reef museum, postcards dropped from 20 percent of items sold to 10 percent. The buyer concludes that fewer postcards were sold.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["The later total was smaller than the earlier total, not merely no larger, for the claim that fewer postcards were sold.","The shop did not sell enough additional other items to offset postcards' smaller share.","The later period included a holiday weekend and the earlier period did not.","Some visitors bought more than one postcard.","Most cases outside this one also fit the claim that fewer postcards were sold, whether or not they share its circumstances."],"a":1,"e":"The credited choice is the one that does the job this question asks. The attractive choice — The later total was smaller than the earlier total, not merely no larger, for the claim that fewer postcards were sold. — loses because it would carry the conclusion if it were granted, but the argument does not need a claim that strong. Sufficient is not the same as necessary."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Weak analogy","d":2,"s":"Requiring a signed clipboard reduced lost tools at the pottery studio. The owner concludes that the same rule will reduce lost tools at the boathouse.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that the same rule will reduce lost tools at the boathouse follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case.","The boathouse is larger than the studio, so a rule that works in a studio will fail in a boathouse.","The boathouse is closer to the water than the studio is.","Tools go missing at the boathouse for the same sort of reason, and at a similar handoff, as at the studio.","The studio started the rule in July."],"a":3,"e":"The credited choice is the one that does the job this question asks. The attractive choice — Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that the same rule will reduce lost tools at the boathouse follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case. — loses because it would carry the conclusion if it were granted, but the argument does not need a claim that strong. Sufficient is not the same as necessary."},
{"t":"Sufficient assumption","pattern":"Percentages vs. numbers","d":3,"s":"Bikes fell from 40 percent of the vehicles in the garage to 25 percent. Therefore, the garage holds fewer bikes.","q":"The conclusion follows logically if which one of the following is assumed?","c":["Most cases that meet the stated condition turn out the way the claim that the garage holds fewer bikes describes, though not all of them do.","Twenty-five percent is a smaller share than 40 percent.","Some bikes are stored on wall racks.","The result comes about only if the stated condition is met.","The garage holds no more vehicles now than it held when bikes were 40 percent."],"a":4,"e":"The credited choice is the one that does the job this question asks. The attractive choice — Most cases that meet the stated condition turn out the way the claim that the garage holds fewer bikes describes, though not all of them do. — loses because \"most,\" or a condition running in the other direction, does not force this conclusion from these premises."},
{"t":"Sufficient assumption","pattern":"Past-to-future","d":3,"s":"The bridge carried each of the last ten harvest floats. Therefore, it will carry this year's float.","q":"The conclusion follows logically if which one of the following is assumed?","c":["If the bridge carried each of the last ten harvest floats, then it will carry this year's float.","Most cases that meet the stated condition turn out the way the claim that it will carry this year's float describes, though not all of them do.","Last year's float arrived on time.","The result comes about only if the stated condition is met.","The premises were recorded accurately, which is all the argument has so far offered for the claim that it will carry this year's float."],"a":0,"e":"The credited choice is the one that does the job this question asks. The attractive choice — Most cases that meet the stated condition turn out the way the claim that it will carry this year's float describes, though not all of them do. — loses because \"most,\" or a condition running in the other direction, does not force this conclusion from these premises."},
{"t":"Sufficient assumption","pattern":"Ad hominem","d":3,"s":"Okada says the bridge should close. Okada's cousin gains boat riders if it closes. Therefore, the bridge need not close.","q":"The conclusion follows logically if which one of the following is assumed?","c":["Most cases that meet the stated condition turn out the way the claim that the bridge need not close describes, though not all of them do.","Whenever a critic's relative would gain from a closing, the structure the critic wants closed need not close.","The result comes about only if the stated condition is met.","The premises were recorded accurately, which is all the argument has so far offered for the claim that the bridge need not close.","Some further condition, never stated, would be enough for a result other than the claim that the bridge need not close."],"a":1,"e":"The credited choice is the one that does the job this question asks. The attractive choice — Most cases that meet the stated condition turn out the way the claim that the bridge need not close describes, though not all of them do. — loses because \"most,\" or a condition running in the other direction, does not force this conclusion from these premises."},
{"t":"Sufficient assumption","pattern":"Appeal to authority","d":3,"s":"A pastry chef says the bridge cables should be replaced. Therefore, the bridge cables should be replaced.","q":"The conclusion follows logically if which one of the following is assumed?","c":["Most cases that meet the stated condition turn out the way the claim that the bridge cables should be replaced describes, though not all of them do.","Cables on this bridge are made of steel.","Whatever this pastry chef says about bridge cables is correct.","Pastry chefs often bake before dawn.","The result comes about only if the stated condition is met."],"a":2,"e":"The credited choice is the one that does the job this question asks. The attractive choice — Most cases that meet the stated condition turn out the way the claim that the bridge cables should be replaced describes, though not all of them do. — loses because \"most,\" or a condition running in the other direction, does not force this conclusion from these premises."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Appeal to authority","d":2,"s":"Dr. Feldman, author of the harbor-piling manual, says the elm pilings should be replaced this year. The board concludes that they should be replaced this year.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The person cited for the claim that they should be replaced this year is known for a neighboring field and never applied this field's standard to this case.","A newspaper printed Feldman's remark without comment.","Several board members have not read the manual.","Feldman's remark applies that manual's measurements and replacement rules to the elm pilings as they stand this year.","What the argument already reports about the claim that they should be replaced this year was recorded by one person, and no rival factor was measured."],"a":3,"e":"A citation helps only when this source applied the relevant standard to this case. The attractive choice — The person cited for the claim that they should be replaced this year is known for a neighboring field and never applied this field's standard to this case. — loses because it is about this comparison, but it makes the conclusion less likely or leaves the gap alone. A fact that cuts against the conclusion is not a strengthener."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Past-to-future","d":2,"s":"The bridge carried every harvest float for a decade. The marshal concludes that it will carry this year's float.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The upcoming case differs, in the respect that mattered, from the past successes cited for the claim that it will carry this year's float.","The parade has used this bridge rather than the main road for the whole decade.","The float is decorated with the same colors it has used for a decade.","What the argument already reports about the claim that it will carry this year's float was recorded by one person, and no rival factor was measured.","This year's float weighs less than every float the bridge has already carried."],"a":4,"e":"A past run supports the next case only when that case is like the ones that worked. The credited fact says it is. The attractive choice — The upcoming case differs, in the respect that mattered, from the past successes cited for the claim that it will carry this year's float. — loses because it is about this comparison, but it makes the conclusion less likely or leaves the gap alone. A fact that cuts against the conclusion is not a strengthener."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Ad hominem","d":2,"s":"The council dismisses Okada's call to close the bridge because Okada's cousin gains boat riders if it closes, and the council concludes the bridge need not close.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["Okada offered no measurement, and an inspection firm with no boat business found the beams sound.","The personal stake is simply larger than the argument already says, and no one has found a separate defect in the work behind the claim that the council dismisses Okada's call to close the bridge because Okada's cousin gains boat riders if.","A second engineer, with no boat business, also recommended closing the bridge.","Some residents prefer the view from the bridge to the view from a boat.","Boat taxis have lost riders over the past year."],"a":0,"e":"What matters is a defect in the work. A motive, by itself, does not supply one. The attractive choice — The personal stake is simply larger than the argument already says, and no one has found a separate defect in the work behind the claim that the council dismisses Okada's call to close the bridge because Okada's cousin gains boat riders if. — loses because it is about this comparison, but it makes the conclusion less likely or leaves the gap alone. A fact that cuts against the conclusion is not a strengthener."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Unrepresentative sample","d":2,"s":"Most east-path maintainers want more gravel. The office concludes that most east-path users want more gravel.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The people who were asked differ from the wider group on the very point at issue in the claim that most east-path users want more gravel.","On age, how they use the path, and opinions about gravel, the maintainers match the users.","The maintainers were surveyed on a rainy afternoon, when gravel washes out.","Most users live near the creek.","What the argument already reports about the claim that most east-path users want more gravel was recorded by one person, and no rival factor was measured."],"a":1,"e":"The conclusion steps from the people asked to a wider group. A match on the point at issue is what makes that step reasonable. The attractive choice — The people who were asked differ from the wider group on the very point at issue in the claim that most east-path users want more gravel. — loses because it is about this comparison, but it makes the conclusion less likely or leaves the gap alone. A fact that cuts against the conclusion is not a strengthener."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Part-to-whole","d":2,"s":"Each pane in the stack is under the weight one clerk can lift. The builder concludes that the whole stack is under that weight.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["Taken together, the parts go past the limit each part meets on its own, in the situation described by the claim that the whole stack is under that weight.","The clerk can lift a single pane onto a cart.","The stack contains only one pane.","What the argument already reports about the claim that the whole stack is under that weight was recorded by one person, and no rival factor was measured.","One side of the comparison in the claim that the whole stack is under that weight paid more for equipment than the other side did."],"a":2,"e":"If the whole does not exceed its most extreme part, that part meeting the limit settles the whole. The attractive choice — Taken together, the parts go past the limit each part meets on its own, in the situation described by the claim that the whole stack is under that weight. — loses because it is about this comparison, but it makes the conclusion less likely or leaves the gap alone. A fact that cuts against the conclusion is not a strengthener."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Past-to-future","d":2,"s":"The bridge carried every harvest float for a decade. Therefore, it will carry this year's float.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["The upcoming case sits inside the range of the earlier successes, and nothing relevant has changed for the claim that it will carry this year's float.","The parade has used this bridge rather than the main road for the whole decade.","The float is decorated with the same colors every year.","This year's float weighs far more than any float the bridge has carried.","The result in the claim that it will carry this year's float was measured with one instrument on every part of the comparison."],"a":3,"e":"The next case is relevantly different, so the past run does not reach it. The attractive choice — The upcoming case sits inside the range of the earlier successes, and nothing relevant has changed for the claim that it will carry this year's float. — loses because it is the kind of fact that supports the conclusion. It runs in the strengthening direction, so it is not what most weakens the argument."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Correlation vs. causation","d":2,"s":"Offices that added lunchtime walks later reported fewer fatigue complaints than offices that did not. The director concludes that the walks reduced fatigue.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["Before the change, the two sides already matched on the result and on the other factors relevant to the claim that the walks reduced fatigue, and the cited change was the only difference.","Some offices without walks improved a little as well.","The result in the claim that the walks reduced fatigue was measured with one instrument on every part of the comparison.","The person drawing the claim that the walks reduced fatigue expected that result before the comparison was made.","The offices that added walks had also cut workloads in half, and the other offices had not."],"a":4,"e":"The conclusion treats one difference as the reason the results diverged. The credited fact gives a rival reason, or shows the gap does not depend on that difference. The attractive choice — Before the change, the two sides already matched on the result and on the other factors relevant to the claim that the walks reduced fatigue, and the cited change was the only difference. — loses because it is the kind of fact that supports the conclusion. It runs in the strengthening direction, so it is not what most weakens the argument."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Absence of evidence","d":2,"s":"No archive inspection has reported silverfish. Therefore, the archive contains no silverfish.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["The inspections opened only the reading room, and silverfish in this archive live in the unopened map drawers.","Any instance of the kind denied in the claim that the archive contains no silverfish would have been entered in the record that was read, and that record is complete.","The inspectors are not trained entomologists.","The result in the claim that the archive contains no silverfish was measured with one instrument on every part of the comparison.","The person drawing the claim that the archive contains no silverfish expected that result before the comparison was made."],"a":0,"e":"The record that would have contained an instance is not the record the argument read. The attractive choice — Any instance of the kind denied in the claim that the archive contains no silverfish would have been entered in the record that was read, and that record is complete. — loses because it is the kind of fact that supports the conclusion. It runs in the strengthening direction, so it is not what most weakens the argument."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Circular reasoning","d":3,"s":"The tide table is reliable because it matches the harbormaster, who is reliable because of the tide table. Therefore, the table's reliability has been established.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["An outside standard, drawn from neither of the two sources, agrees with the claim that the table's reliability has been established.","Neither the table nor the harbormaster has ever been checked against anything but the other.","The table and the harbormaster agree with each other.","The result in the claim that the table's reliability has been established was measured with one instrument on every part of the comparison.","The person drawing the claim that the table's reliability has been established expected that result before the comparison was made."],"a":1,"e":"An independent check that disagrees shows the circled sources are a poor ground. The attractive choice — An outside standard, drawn from neither of the two sources, agrees with the claim that the table's reliability has been established. — loses because it is the kind of fact that supports the conclusion. It runs in the strengthening direction, so it is not what most weakens the argument."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Sufficient/necessary confusion","d":2,"s":"Every January rental is winterized. Cabin 7 is winterized. Therefore, cabin 7 is rented in January.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["That condition is the only way to reach the result, and it was met in the case the claim that cabin 7 is rented in January describes.","Cabins that are not winterized are rented in July.","Cabin 7 was winterized for its owner's private use and is not offered for rent in January.","Cabin 7 is the only winterized cabin on the lake.","The result in the claim that cabin 7 is rented in January was measured with one instrument on every part of the comparison."],"a":2,"e":"There is another way to get the result, so the condition the argument cites is not required. The attractive choice — That condition is the only way to reach the result, and it was met in the case the claim that cabin 7 is rented in January describes. — loses because it is the kind of fact that supports the conclusion. It runs in the strengthening direction, so it is not what most weakens the argument."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Appeal to authority","d":2,"s":"A celebrated pastry chef says the bridge cables should be replaced. Therefore, they should be replaced.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["The person cited applied this field's own standard to this case before the claim that they should be replaced was drawn.","The chef lives in the same city as the bridge.","The result in the claim that they should be replaced was measured with one instrument on every part of the comparison.","Bridge engineers examined these cables and concluded they should stay in place.","The person drawing the claim that they should be replaced expected that result before the comparison was made."],"a":3,"e":"A source applying the proper standard points the other way, which undercuts a bare citation. The attractive choice — The person cited applied this field's own standard to this case before the claim that they should be replaced was drawn. — loses because it is the kind of fact that supports the conclusion. It runs in the strengthening direction, so it is not what most weakens the argument."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Conditional reversal/negation","d":3,"s":"If a crate is stored in the hill warehouse, then it is logged in the red book. Crate K is not logged in the red book. Crate M is logged in the red book.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["Crate M is stored in the hill warehouse.","Crate K is logged in some book other than the red book.","No crate logged in the red book is stored in the hill warehouse.","Crate M is not stored in the hill warehouse.","Crate K is not stored in the hill warehouse."],"a":4,"e":"Storage is sufficient for a red-book line, so a crate with no such line was not stored. That is K. M's line leaves M's location open. The attractive choice — Crate M is stored in the hill warehouse. — loses because a red-book line is necessary for hill-warehouse storage, not sufficient. M's line is what a stored crate would have. It does not show M was stored. K's missing line does show K was not stored."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Ignoring alternatives","d":2,"s":"The oven stopped heating. A burned-out element, a tripped breaker, or a gas shutoff could each stop it. The notes do not say which of those occurred.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["The notes do not establish whether the element, the breaker, or the gas shutoff stopped the oven.","The result holds because of those facts, and it would disappear if any one of them were removed, beyond what the claim that the oven stopped heating requires.","None of the three causes could stop this oven.","The oven stopped because of a gas shutoff.","The same link will hold next year in every case like the claim that the oven stopped heating."],"a":0,"e":"The credited statement is forced by the statements. The tempting one adds a cause, a reversed link, or a claim about other cases. The attractive choice — The result holds because of those facts, and it would disappear if any one of them were removed, beyond what the claim that the oven stopped heating requires. — loses because it adds a cause, a next-year guarantee, or a reversed link. The statements support something narrower."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Circular reasoning","d":3,"s":"A remark that offers a claim as its only ground does not establish that claim. Oren's only ground for \"the bell is on time\" is that the bell is on time.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["The result holds because of those facts, and it would disappear if any one of them were removed, beyond what the claim that a remark that offers a claim as its only ground does not establish that claim requires.","Oren's remark does not establish that the bell is on time.","Any remark about the bell is circular.","The same link will hold next year in every case like the claim that a remark that offers a claim as its only ground does not establish that claim.","Most of a second group, not described here, also fall under the claim that a remark that offers a claim as its only ground does not establish that claim."],"a":1,"e":"The credited statement is forced by the statements. The tempting one adds a cause, a reversed link, or a claim about other cases. The attractive choice — The result holds because of those facts, and it would disappear if any one of them were removed, beyond what the claim that a remark that offers a claim as its only ground does not establish that claim requires. — loses because it adds a cause, a next-year guarantee, or a reversed link. The statements support something narrower."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Weak analogy","d":2,"s":"A result in one setting supports the same result in a second setting only if the settings are alike in the way that matters. A phone ban reduced noise in a reading room. Nothing is said about whether ward noise has that same source.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["The reading room and the wards are alike in the way that matters, as the argument states it.","A phone ban will not reduce noise in the wards.","The reading-room result does not, by itself, show that a phone ban will reduce noise in the wards.","The reading-room ban failed to reduce noise.","Ward noise is mostly from phones."],"a":2,"e":"The credited statement is forced by the statements. The tempting one adds a cause, a reversed link, or a claim about other cases. The attractive choice — The reading room and the wards are alike in the way that matters, as the argument states it. — loses because it adds a cause, a next-year guarantee, or a reversed link. The statements support something narrower."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Conditional chain","d":2,"s":"Every shuttle that stops at the pier collects a token. Every shuttle that collects a token is logged before dusk. The north shuttle was not logged before dusk. The south shuttle collected a token.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["The south shuttle stopped at the pier.","The north shuttle did not stop at the pier.","The south shuttle was not logged before dusk.","The north shuttle collected a token.","No shuttle that collects a token stops at the pier."],"a":1,"e":"A pier stop would collect a token and then be logged. The north shuttle was not logged, so it did not stop at the pier. The south shuttle's token does not place it at the pier. The attractive choice — The south shuttle stopped at the pier. — loses because a token is necessary for a pier stop only in the sense that a pier stop produces a token. Collecting a token does not show the south shuttle stopped at the pier, because the rule does not say tokens come only from the pier. No log does show the north shuttle missed the pier."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Past-to-future","d":2,"s":"The kiln reached its target on all twelve firings this month, each with the same clay, load, and fuel. The next firing is planned with those same three settings. No one claims the kiln cannot fail.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["The clay, rather than the fuel, caused every past success, as the argument states it.","Fuel settings do not affect kiln temperature.","The next firing must reach the target.","There is reason to expect the next firing, so planned, to reach the target, though that result is not guaranteed.","The next firing will miss the target."],"a":3,"e":"The credited statement stays inside what the information supports. The attractive choice — The clay, rather than the fuel, caused every past success, as the argument states it. — loses because it adds a cause, a next-year guarantee, or a reversed link. The statements support something narrower."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Weak analogy","d":2,"s":"Badges are lost at an unattended desk at both the craft museum and the science hall, the desks are staffed in the same way, and a sign-in clipboard cut those losses at the craft museum.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["The two halls differ in every way that matters to lost badges, as the argument states it.","The craft museum result is irrelevant to the science hall.","The science hall will have no lost badges at all.","Clipboards cause visitors to lose badges.","There is reason to expect a similar clipboard to cut that same kind of loss at the science hall."],"a":4,"e":"The credited statement stays inside what the information supports. The attractive choice — The two halls differ in every way that matters to lost badges, as the argument states it. — loses because it adds a cause, a next-year guarantee, or a reversed link. The statements support something narrower."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"False dichotomy","d":2,"s":"The orchard will not use netting. It will use reflective tape, and that tape has kept similar crops from being ruined. No other threat to this crop is mentioned.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["Skipping netting does not, on these facts, show that the crop will be ruined.","The result holds because of those facts, and it would disappear if any one of them were removed, beyond what the claim that the orchard will not use netting requires.","The orchard will use netting after all.","Birds are never a threat to fruit.","The same link will hold next year in every case like the claim that the orchard will not use netting."],"a":0,"e":"The credited statement stays inside what the information supports. The attractive choice — The result holds because of those facts, and it would disappear if any one of them were removed, beyond what the claim that the orchard will not use netting requires. — loses because it adds a cause, a next-year guarantee, or a reversed link. The statements support something narrower."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Ad hominem","d":2,"s":"An inspection firm with no boat business found cracked supports and recommended closing the bridge. The council's only reply is that the engineer who first raised the alarm has a cousin who gains riders if the bridge closes.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["The result holds because of those facts, and it would disappear if any one of them were removed, beyond what the claim that an inspection firm with no boat business found cracked supports and recommended closing the bridge requires.","No bridge with a cracked support should ever be repaired.","The cousin's gain does not answer the inspection firm's finding.","The inspection firm also gains boat riders.","The same link will hold next year in every case like the claim that an inspection firm with no boat business found cracked supports and recommended closing the bridge."],"a":2,"e":"The credited statement stays inside what the information supports. The attractive choice — The result holds because of those facts, and it would disappear if any one of them were removed, beyond what the claim that an inspection firm with no boat business found cracked supports and recommended closing the bridge requires. — loses because it adds a cause, a next-year guarantee, or a reversed link. The statements support something narrower."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Unrepresentative sample","d":2,"s":"Maintainers and users of the east path match on age, on how they use the path, and on stated views about gravel. Most of the maintainers want more gravel.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["The result holds because of those facts, and it would disappear if any one of them were removed, beyond what the claim that maintainers and users of the east path match on age, on how they use the path, requires.","Gravel is cheaper than wood, so users must want it.","The maintainers were never asked.","There is reason to think most users want more gravel.","The same link will hold next year in every case like the claim that maintainers and users of the east path match on age, on how they use the path,."],"a":3,"e":"The credited statement stays inside what the information supports. The attractive choice — The result holds because of those facts, and it would disappear if any one of them were removed, beyond what the claim that maintainers and users of the east path match on age, on how they use the path, requires. — loses because it adds a cause, a next-year guarantee, or a reversed link. The statements support something narrower."},
{"t":"Main point","pattern":"Main conclusion","d":1,"s":"Honey sales and the new market rose in the same weeks. The clerk treats that overlap as proof that the market caused the rise. Overlap, by itself, is not proof of a cause.","q":"Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?","c":["It is not the case that honey sales and the new market rose in the same weeks.","Markets always cause honey sales to rise.","The right response is to act at once on the claim that honey sales and the new market rose in the same weeks and to treat every similar case the same way.","The evidence offered for the claim that honey sales and the new market rose in the same weeks was gathered by the person who states it.","The clerk has not proved that the market caused the rise in honey sales."],"a":4,"e":"The main conclusion is the claim the other statements are offered to support. The attractive choice — It is not the case that honey sales and the new market rose in the same weeks. — loses because it is a premise, the denial of the conclusion, or a recommendation the argument does not draw."},
{"t":"Main point","pattern":"Main conclusion","d":1,"s":"Green badges of this kind go only to people who finish the dock course. Riley holds one. So Riley finished the dock course.","q":"Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?","c":["Riley finished the dock course.","Green badges of this kind go only to people who finish the dock course.","Every person who finished the course holds a green badge.","The right response is to act at once on the claim that Riley finished the dock course and to treat every similar case the same way.","The evidence offered for the claim that Riley finished the dock course was gathered by the person who states it."],"a":0,"e":"The main conclusion is the claim the other statements are offered to support. The attractive choice — Green badges of this kind go only to people who finish the dock course. — loses because it is a premise, the denial of the conclusion, or a recommendation the argument does not draw."},
{"t":"Main point","pattern":"Main conclusion","d":1,"s":"The garage holds the same number of vehicles as last year, and bikes' share fell from 40 percent to 25 percent. Therefore the garage holds fewer bikes.","q":"Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?","c":["Bikes' share of the vehicles fell from 40 percent to 25 percent.","The garage holds fewer bikes.","The garage holds the same number of vehicles as last year.","Forty percent is a larger share than 25 percent.","The right response is to act at once on the claim that the garage holds fewer bikes and to treat every similar case the same way."],"a":1,"e":"The main conclusion is the claim the other statements are offered to support. The attractive choice — Bikes' share of the vehicles fell from 40 percent to 25 percent. — loses because it is a premise, the denial of the conclusion, or a recommendation the argument does not draw."},
{"t":"Main point","pattern":"Main conclusion","d":1,"s":"This year's float is far heavier than any float the bridge has carried. A decade of lighter floats therefore does not show that the bridge will carry this year's float.","q":"Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?","c":["It is not the case that this year's float is far heavier than any float the bridge has carried.","The bridge carried ten years of floats, so it will carry this one.","A decade of lighter floats does not show that the bridge will carry this year's float.","This year's float is far heavier than earlier floats.","The bridge will collapse under this year's float."],"a":2,"e":"The main conclusion is the claim the other statements are offered to support. The attractive choice — It is not the case that this year's float is far heavier than any float the bridge has carried. — loses because it is a premise, the denial of the conclusion, or a recommendation the argument does not draw."},
{"t":"Method","pattern":"Argument method","d":2,"s":"Okada's cousin gains boat riders if the bridge closes. Citing only that, the council dismisses Okada's warning and concludes the bridge need not close.","q":"The argument proceeds by","c":["citing an independent inspection that found the beams sound, as the argument states it","treating each light case as proof the set of cases is light","showing that netting is not the only way to save a crop","rejecting a warning because of a relative's stake rather than because of evidence about the bridge","defining \"original\" in two ways"],"a":3,"e":"The credited description is the move the argument makes. The attractive choice — citing an independent inspection that found the beams sound, as the argument states it — loses because it names a real technique, but not the one this argument uses."},
{"t":"Method","pattern":"Argument method","d":2,"s":"Each map folds into a pocket, so the packet of maps folds into a pocket.","q":"The argument proceeds by","c":["treating a condition needed to fold a map as sufficient for being allowed on the trail","turning a smaller percentage of maps into a smaller number without a total","projecting next weekend's weather from a dry month","treating a condition required for the result as though it guaranteed the claim that each map folds into a pocket, so the packet of maps folds into a pocket","attributing to a collection a feature established only for its members"],"a":4,"e":"The credited description is the move the argument makes. The attractive choice — treating a condition needed to fold a map as sufficient for being allowed on the trail — loses because it names a real technique, but not the one this argument uses."},
{"t":"Method","pattern":"Argument method","d":2,"s":"Bikes' share of the garage fell, and the total number of vehicles is not discussed. The buyer concludes that the garage holds fewer bikes.","q":"The argument proceeds by","c":["attacking the buyer's motive for preferring cars","using \"bike\" in a mechanical sense and then in a legal sense","inferring a drop in number from a drop in share alone","concluding the fountain is quiet because each pump is, while equating the fountain with its loudest pump","citing last summer as the only ground for this summer"],"a":2,"e":"The share falls and the count is concluded, with no total. That is the percentage-to-number move. The attractive choice — attacking the buyer's motive for preferring cars — loses because no motive is mentioned. The attractive choice hears a buyer and invents an ad hominem. Share is being read as number."},
{"t":"Method","pattern":"Argument method","d":2,"s":"Riley has the badge that finishing the course is sufficient to earn. Therefore, Riley finished the course.","q":"The argument proceeds by","c":["treating a sufficient condition for the badge as though it were a necessary one","polling only donors and concluding about all visitors","weighing this year's float against a decade of lighter ones","matching an outside gauge to a tide table","oiling a bell and then inferring a cause from the next day's lateness in this instance"],"a":0,"e":"The course is enough for the badge, and the badge is then treated as enough for the course. That reverses a sufficient condition. The attractive choice — polling only donors and concluding about all visitors — loses because there is no poll. The attractive choice is a sample flaw with none of the argument's people except a vague \"therefore.\" The move is a one-way badge rule run backward."},
{"t":"Role","pattern":"Statement role","d":1,"s":"**Okada's cousin gains boat riders if the bridge closes.** Citing only that, the council dismisses Okada's warning.","q":"The statement in bold plays which one of the following roles in the argument?","c":["It is an independent inspection result about the beams in this instance.","It is the conclusion that the bridge is safe.","It is a definition of \"cousin.\"","It is the sole ground offered for dismissing the warning.","It is a concession that the beams are cracked."],"a":3,"e":"The council cites that fact alone. It is support for the dismissal, not the conclusion that the bridge is safe and not an inspection. The attractive choice — It is an independent inspection result about the beams in this instance. — loses because the cousin's gain feels like a finding and is easy to promote into an inspection. No beams are measured in that sentence. It is the reason given for the dismissal, not a structural report."},
{"t":"Role","pattern":"Statement role","d":1,"s":"Each case is light enough to carry. **The full set is light enough for one person to carry.** That is what the quartet concludes.","q":"The statement in bold plays which one of the following roles in the argument?","c":["It is the premise about each case.","It is the conclusion of the argument.","It is an objection by the streetcar conductor.","It is a definition of \"quartet.\"","It is evidence that one case is heavy."],"a":1,"e":"The quartet concludes the bold sentence. The cases one by one are the support. The attractive choice — It is the premise about each case. — loses because both sentences mention carrying, so the set's sentence is easy to file with the premise about the cases. The argument says the set's sentence is the conclusion. The premise is the sentence about each case."},
{"t":"Role","pattern":"Statement role","d":1,"s":"Bikes' share fell from 40 percent to 25 percent. **The garage holds fewer bikes.** The buyer concludes that from the share drop alone.","q":"The statement in bold plays which one of the following roles in the argument?","c":["It is the premise about the percentages.","It is an objection offered by a mechanic.","It is a definition of \"garage.\"","It is evidence that the total number of vehicles doubled.","It is the conclusion."],"a":4,"e":"The buyer concludes the bold sentence from the share drop. The share drop is the premise. The attractive choice — It is the premise about the percentages. — loses because fewer bikes is the arithmetical-looking sentence, so it is easy to treat it as the percentage premise. The percentage premise is the earlier sentence. Fewer bikes is what the buyer concludes."},
{"t":"Principle apply","pattern":"Applying a principle","d":1,"s":"Principle: a museum may sell a donated work only if the donor agreed to sales and the work is a duplicate. The Reed sketch was donated with no agreement about sales, and it is a duplicate. The Lau print is a duplicate and its donor agreed to sales.","q":"Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle?","c":["The museum must sell the Lau print.","The museum may sell the Reed sketch.","The principle does not permit selling the Reed sketch.","The principle does not permit selling the Lau print.","The museum may sell every duplicate, including works whose donors refused sales."],"a":2,"e":"Selling the Reed sketch would violate a necessary condition. The principle therefore does not permit that sale. It does not order the Lau sale. The attractive choice — The museum must sell the Lau print. — loses because the Lau print meets both necessary conditions, so a sale is not forbidden. \"Only if\" does not create a duty to sell. \"Must sell\" turns a failed ban into an order. The Reed sketch lacks the donor's agreement, so a sale of it is not permitted."},
{"t":"Principle apply","pattern":"Applying a principle","d":1,"s":"Principle: an endorsement establishes a cable replacement only if the endorser applies a relevant engineering standard to those cables. A pastry chef, applying no engineering standard, says these cables should be replaced. That remark is the only endorsement.","q":"Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle?","c":["The remark does not establish that the cables should be replaced.","No engineer could establish a cable replacement either in this instance.","The cables should be replaced.","The chef applied an engineering standard.","The cables should be removed tomorrow morning."],"a":0,"e":"The only endorsement applies no relevant standard, so it does not establish replacement. The principle does not decide what an engineer could show. The attractive choice — No engineer could establish a cable replacement either in this instance. — loses because the principle withholds establishment from endorsements that skip the standard. It does not say engineers are powerless. The chef skipped the standard. An engineer who applied it is outside the principle's ban."},
{"t":"Principle apply","pattern":"Applying a principle","d":1,"s":"Principle: silence in a log establishes absence only if any instance would have been logged and the log is complete. Every jam would have been logged, the log is complete, and no jam is logged.","q":"Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle?","c":["The silence establishes nothing about jams.","The silence establishes that there was no jam.","There was a jam that the log missed.","The result in the claim that silence in a log establishes absence only if any instance would have been logged was already in place before the factor the argument relies on.","A further record of the claim that silence in a log establishes absence only if any instance would have been logged was kept by the same office across the period the argument discusses."],"a":1,"e":"Applied as written, the principle yields the credited judgment and not the tempting one. The attractive choice — The silence establishes nothing about jams. — loses because it is a judgment in the same setting, and the principle as stated does not yield it."},
{"t":"Principle apply","pattern":"Applying a principle","d":1,"s":"Principle: a claim offered as its own only ground is not adequate evidence for that claim. Oren's only ground for \"the bell is on time\" is that the bell is on time.","q":"Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle?","c":["The bell is late.","Oren has not given adequate evidence that the bell is on time.","The bell is on time.","Oren checked a second clock.","Every statement about bells is circular, as the argument states it."],"a":1,"e":"Oren's only ground is the claim itself, so the principle says the evidence is not adequate. The bell's actual time is not decided. The attractive choice — The bell is late. — loses because the principle grades the evidence, not the bell. \"Late\" is a factual verdict the evidence's emptiness does not deliver."},
{"t":"Principle apply","pattern":"Applying a principle","d":1,"s":"Principle: if this year's float is heavier than any float the bridge has carried, a decade of success does not establish that the bridge will carry this year's float. This year's float is heavier than any of those floats.","q":"Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle?","c":["Decades of success always transfer to heavier loads.","This year's float is lighter than the earlier ones.","The bridge will carry the float.","The decade does not establish that the bridge will carry this year's float.","The result in the claim that if this year's float is heavier than any float the bridge has carried was already in place before the factor the argument relies on."],"a":3,"e":"Applied as written, the principle yields the credited judgment and not the tempting one. The attractive choice — Decades of success always transfer to heavier loads. — loses because it is a judgment in the same setting, and the principle as stated does not yield it."},
{"t":"Principle identify","pattern":"Identifying a principle","d":1,"s":"The council cites only the cousin's gain and dismisses the warning. That citation does not show the bridge need not close, because a motive is not evidence about the beams.","q":"The argument most closely conforms to which one of the following principles?","c":["Any warning from someone with a relative in a related business is false, as the argument states it.","Bridges should close whenever a cousin gains riders.","Family ties are the best evidence about beams.","Councils should ignore inspections.","A motive for offering a warning does not, by itself, show that the warned-about step is unnecessary."],"a":4,"e":"The credited principle is the rule the argument is using. The attractive choice — Any warning from someone with a relative in a related business is false, as the argument states it. — loses because it is a nearby rule, often a stronger or reversed cousin of the rule the argument actually uses."},
{"t":"Principle identify","pattern":"Identifying a principle","d":1,"s":"Each case is light, and nothing is said about the combined weight. So the quartet has not shown that one person can carry the set.","q":"The argument most closely conforms to which one of the following principles?","c":["A feature of each member does not, by itself, establish that same feature for the collection.","Whatever is true of each member is true of the collection, as the argument states it.","Light cases cannot be carried on a streetcar.","One heavy member makes every member heavy.","Quartets travel only by streetcar."],"a":0,"e":"The credited principle is the rule the argument is using. The attractive choice — Whatever is true of each member is true of the collection, as the argument states it. — loses because it is a nearby rule, often a stronger or reversed cousin of the rule the argument actually uses."},
{"t":"Principle identify","pattern":"Identifying a principle","d":1,"s":"Bikes' share fell, and the total number of vehicles is unknown. So it has not been shown that there are fewer bikes.","q":"The argument most closely conforms to which one of the following principles?","c":["The person who counted the bikes also counted the cars.","A smaller share always means a smaller number.","A smaller share does not establish a smaller number when the total may have grown enough to offset it.","Percentages are never about counts.","If the facts cited for the claim that it has not been shown that there are fewer bikes merely occur together, that claim is established."],"a":2,"e":"The credited principle is the rule the argument is using. The attractive choice — The person who counted the bikes also counted the cars. — loses because it is a nearby rule, often a stronger or reversed cousin of the rule the argument actually uses."},
{"t":"Principle identify","pattern":"Identifying a principle","d":1,"s":"This kind of badge is issued only to people who finish the dock course, and Riley holds one. So Riley finished the course.","q":"The argument most closely conforms to which one of the following principles?","c":["If a condition is sufficient for a result, then anyone who has the result met the condition in this instance.","If a result is produced only by a certain condition, then anyone who has the result met the condition.","Badges prove nothing about courses.","Inspectors receive every badge.","Courses are unnecessary for badges."],"a":1,"e":"The badge comes only from finishing, so holding it yields finishing. That is the \"only if\" principle, not the reversed sufficient-condition fallacy. The attractive choice — If a condition is sufficient for a result, then anyone who has the result met the condition in this instance. — loses because the argument uses \"only,\" which runs from the badge back to the course. The attractive principle runs a merely sufficient condition backward, which is the mistake this argument avoids. The wording is nearly the same and the logical direction is not."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":2,"s":"The clinic that added hand-washing stations recorded more infections the next month, not fewer.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["The step that was supposed to prevent the surprise was carried out in full, and people complied with it, during the same period as the claim that the clinic that added hand-washing stations recorded more infections the next month, not fewer.","Staff were told to wash their hands more often, and audits showed they did.","The clinic treated the same number of patients as in the previous month.","The new stations came with a test that detects mild infections the old test missed, and the underlying infection rate fell.","Hand-washing stations are recommended by several hospital manuals."],"a":3,"e":"The credited fact is the missing piece that lets both halves of the story be true together. The attractive choice — The step that was supposed to prevent the surprise was carried out in full, and people complied with it, during the same period as the claim that the clinic that added hand-washing stations recorded more infections the next month, not fewer. — loses because it confirms that the surprising half really happened, or it describes a different setting. It does not let both halves of this story stand together."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":2,"s":"After the ferry added a dawn trip, it carried fewer passengers per week than before the trip was added.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["The step that was supposed to prevent the surprise was carried out in full, and people complied with it, during the same period as the claim that after the ferry added a dawn trip, it carried fewer passengers per week than before the trip was.","Fares were the same on the dawn trip as on the midday trips.","The dawn trip was advertised in the weekly rider bulletin.","The ferry's seating capacity was not reduced.","The dawn trip replaced two busier midday trips rather than being added on top of them."],"a":4,"e":"The credited fact is the missing piece that lets both halves of the story be true together. The attractive choice — The step that was supposed to prevent the surprise was carried out in full, and people complied with it, during the same period as the claim that after the ferry added a dawn trip, it carried fewer passengers per week than before the trip was. — loses because it confirms that the surprising half really happened, or it describes a different setting. It does not let both halves of this story stand together."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":2,"s":"The town that added evening streetlights had more reported evening collisions that season than in the previous, darker season.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["The new lights came with a rule that residents report every minor scrape, and minor scrapes had gone unreported before.","The step that was supposed to prevent the surprise was carried out in full, and people complied with it, during the same period as the claim that the town that added evening streetlights had more reported evening collisions that season than in.","Traffic volume on the lighted streets was about the same as in the darker season.","The lights met the brightness level the town's traffic office requested.","Police staffing on those streets did not increase."],"a":0,"e":"The credited fact is the missing piece that lets both halves of the story be true together. The attractive choice — The step that was supposed to prevent the surprise was carried out in full, and people complied with it, during the same period as the claim that the town that added evening streetlights had more reported evening collisions that season than in. — loses because it confirms that the surprising half really happened, or it describes a different setting. It does not let both halves of this story stand together."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":2,"s":"The garden that watered more often had drier soil at dawn than the garden that watered less.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["The step that was supposed to prevent the surprise was carried out in full, and people complied with it, during the same period as the claim that the garden that watered more often had drier soil at dawn than the garden that watered less.","The frequently watered garden received more gallons per week than the other garden.","The frequent watering was done at noon onto covered beds that shed the water, while the other garden watered the soil itself at dusk.","One garden was watered more often than the other.","The claim that the garden that watered more often had drier soil at dawn than the garden that watered less was measured the same way before and after the change."],"a":2,"e":"The credited fact is the missing piece that lets both halves of the story be true together. The attractive choice — The step that was supposed to prevent the surprise was carried out in full, and people complied with it, during the same period as the claim that the garden that watered more often had drier soil at dawn than the garden that watered less. — loses because it confirms that the surprising half really happened, or it describes a different setting. It does not let both halves of this story stand together."},
{"t":"Parallel","pattern":"Parallel reasoning","d":3,"s":"Each pump is under the limit, and the fountain is only as loud as its loudest pump. Therefore, the fountain is under the limit.","q":"Which one of the following is most similar in its reasoning to the argument above?","c":["Each pane is under the lift limit, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, a stack of ten panes is under the lift limit.","The stack is under the lift limit, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, each pane is under it.","Panes are glass, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, the greenhouse is glass.","The limit was set by the council, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, the fountain is quiet.","Each pane is under the lift limit, and the stack contains only one pane. Therefore, the stack is under the lift limit."],"a":4,"e":"Both add a premise that stops the whole from outrunning the parts, and only then conclude about the whole. The attractive choice — Each pane is under the lift limit, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, a stack of ten panes is under the lift limit. — loses because the trap drops the extra premise that keeps the whole from exceeding the parts. The stimulus keeps that premise, and so does the one-pane stack. Ten panes without that premise is the flawed version."},
{"t":"Parallel","pattern":"Parallel reasoning","d":3,"s":"It did not rain, the path drains quickly, it was underwater, and an open line is the only other way it floods. Therefore, the line was left open.","q":"Which one of the following is most similar in its reasoning to the argument above?","c":["The lamp is dark, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, the bulb burned out on those facts alone.","The breaker tripped, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, the lamp is dark on those facts alone.","The lamp was not unplugged, the bulb is new, the lamp is dark, and a tripped breaker is the only other way this lamp goes dark. Therefore, the breaker tripped.","It did not rain, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, no path can be underwater on those facts alone.","Bulbs are glass, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, breakers trip on those facts alone."],"a":2,"e":"Both eliminate every listed cause but one and then conclude that the remaining cause occurred. The attractive choice — The lamp is dark, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, the bulb burned out on those facts alone. — loses because darkness alone picks a cause. The stimulus first removes the other listed causes. The breaker choice does that removal. The burned-out bulb does not."},
{"t":"Parallel","pattern":"Parallel reasoning","d":3,"s":"Reading-room noise was phone audio, and a ban reduced it. Ward noise is monitors, and phones there are already silent. Therefore, the reading-room result is a poor reason to expect quieter wards.","q":"Which one of the following is most similar in its reasoning to the argument above?","c":["Grit fixed the path, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, grit will fix the track on those facts alone.","Phone bans always quiet hospitals. No other support is offered, so the result is taken to follow in every later case of that kind on those facts alone.","The result in the claim that the reading-room result is a poor reason to expect quieter wards was already in place before the factor the argument relies on. No other support is offered, so the result is taken to follow in every later case of that kind.","Path puddles come from packed soil, and grit fixed them. Track puddles come from a missing drain, and grit does not add a drain. Therefore, the path result is a poor reason to expect a dry track.","A further record of the claim that the reading-room result is a poor reason to expect quieter wards was kept by the same office across the period the argument discusses, as the argument states it."],"a":3,"e":"The credited argument matches the original structure, including the direction of the condition. The attractive choice — Grit fixed the path, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, grit will fix the track on those facts alone. — loses because it shares a topic or a few words, but not the structure, or it repairs the mistake."},
{"t":"Parallel flaw","pattern":"Parallel flaw","d":3,"s":"Every guide who may lead night hikes has completed the radio check. Nia has completed the radio check. Therefore, Nia may lead night hikes.","q":"Which one of the following exhibits flawed reasoning most similar to that in the argument above?","c":["Every clerk who may issue a tide permit has passed the chart quiz. Luis has not passed the chart quiz. Therefore, Luis may not issue a tide permit.","If a clerk may issue a tide permit, then that clerk has passed the chart quiz. Luis may issue a tide permit. Therefore, Luis has passed the chart quiz.","Some people who pass the chart quiz are not clerks. Luis passed the quiz, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, Luis is not a clerk.","Every clerk who may issue a tide permit has passed the chart quiz. Luis has passed the chart quiz. Therefore, Luis may issue a tide permit.","No one who failed the chart quiz may issue a tide permit. Luis failed the quiz, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, Luis may not issue a permit."],"a":3,"e":"Both treat a necessary condition as sufficient: the check, or the quiz, is had, and the permission is inferred. The attractive choice — Every clerk who may issue a tide permit has passed the chart quiz. Luis has not passed the chart quiz. Therefore, Luis may not issue a tide permit. — loses because the trap is valid: it denies the necessary condition and denies the permission. The stimulus treats the necessary radio check as if it were sufficient for leading, and the Luis choice that infers the permit from the quiz does the same. Denying the quiz is not that flaw."},
{"t":"Parallel flaw","pattern":"Parallel flaw","d":3,"s":"The matinee audience liked the play. Therefore, nearly all theatergoers like the play.","q":"Which one of the following exhibits flawed reasoning most similar to that in the argument above?","c":["Shoppers already in the premium aisle liked the new tea. Therefore, all of the grocery's customers like the new tea.","A sample that matches the store's customers in age and habits liked the tea. Therefore, there is reason to think the customers like the tea.","Either customers like the tea or the shop will close. They do not like the tea. Therefore, the shop will close.","The tea sold out, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, it was displayed in the premium aisle.","The result in the claim that nearly all theatergoers like the play was already in place before the factor the argument relies on."],"a":0,"e":"The credited argument repeats the same kind of mistake. The attractive choice — A sample that matches the store's customers in age and habits liked the tea. Therefore, there is reason to think the customers like the tea. — loses because it shares a topic or a few words, but not the structure, or it repairs the mistake."},
{"t":"Evaluate","pattern":"Evaluating an argument","d":2,"s":"Offices that added walks later had fewer fatigue complaints. The director concludes that the walks caused the improvement.","q":"Which one of the following would be most useful to know in order to evaluate the argument?","c":["Whether the person drawing the claim that the walks caused the improvement would gain personally if that claim were accepted.","Whether the walk offices also cut workloads, or otherwise changed the afternoons, while the other offices did not","Whether the word \"fatigue\" has more than two syllables","Whether a written notice of the claim that the walks caused the improvement was posted where the audience could read it.","Whether acting on the claim that the walks caused the improvement would be expensive if the claim turned out to be wrong."],"a":1,"e":"The useful question is the one whose answers pull the conclusion in opposite directions. The credited question is aimed at the gap. The attractive choice — Whether the person drawing the claim that the walks caused the improvement would gain personally if that claim were accepted. — loses because it can be answered either way without deciding whether the evidence reaches the conclusion."},
{"t":"Evaluate","pattern":"Evaluating an argument","d":2,"s":"Bikes' share fell from 40 percent to 25 percent. The buyer concludes that the garage holds fewer bikes.","q":"Which one of the following would be most useful to know in order to evaluate the argument?","c":["Whether the person drawing the claim that the garage holds fewer bikes would gain personally if that claim were accepted.","Whether a written notice of the claim that the garage holds fewer bikes was posted where the audience could read it.","Whether acting on the claim that the garage holds fewer bikes would be expensive if the claim turned out to be wrong.","Whether a similar claim was accepted last year in a setting this argument does not discuss.","Whether the total number of vehicles rose enough to offset the smaller share"],"a":4,"e":"The useful question is the one whose answers pull the conclusion in opposite directions. The credited question is aimed at the gap. The attractive choice — Whether the person drawing the claim that the garage holds fewer bikes would gain personally if that claim were accepted. — loses because it can be answered either way without deciding whether the evidence reaches the conclusion."},
{"t":"Evaluate","pattern":"Evaluating an argument","d":2,"s":"Finishing the dock course is enough to earn a green badge. Riley has a green badge. The clerk concludes that Riley finished the course.","q":"Which one of the following would be most useful to know in order to evaluate the argument?","c":["Whether green badges are issued in any way other than finishing the course","Whether the person drawing the claim that Riley finished the course would gain personally if that claim were accepted.","Whether the course was once longer than it is now","Whether a written notice of the claim that Riley finished the course was posted where the audience could read it.","Whether acting on the claim that Riley finished the course would be expensive if the claim turned out to be wrong."],"a":0,"e":"The useful question is the one whose answers pull the conclusion in opposite directions. The credited question is aimed at the gap. The attractive choice — Whether the person drawing the claim that Riley finished the course would gain personally if that claim were accepted. — loses because it can be answered either way without deciding whether the evidence reaches the conclusion."},
{"t":"Evaluate","pattern":"Evaluating an argument","d":2,"s":"The path was underwater. The groundskeeper concludes that the irrigation line was left open.","q":"Which one of the following would be most useful to know in order to evaluate the argument?","c":["Whether the person drawing the claim that the irrigation line was left open would gain personally if that claim were accepted.","Whether rain or another cause, with the valve closed, left the path underwater","Whether the groundskeeper prefers irrigation to watering by hand","Whether the groundskeeper has held the job for many years","Whether a written notice of the claim that the irrigation line was left open was posted where the audience could read it."],"a":1,"e":"The useful question is the one whose answers pull the conclusion in opposite directions. The credited question is aimed at the gap. The attractive choice — Whether the person drawing the claim that the irrigation line was left open would gain personally if that claim were accepted. — loses because it can be answered either way without deciding whether the evidence reaches the conclusion."},
{"t":"Point at issue","pattern":"Point at issue","d":3,"s":"Pilar: The elm footbridge should be closed, because its beams are cracked. Rafael: The beams are cracked, but the bridge should stay open, because a posted weight limit would keep the heavy floats off it.","q":"The statements provide the most support for the claim that the two speakers disagree over whether","c":["the footbridge both speakers mention was actually under discussion","one of the two speakers has a personal stake in how the question about the footbridge comes out","the elm footbridge should be closed","every dispute of this kind has to be resolved the way the speakers resolve the footbridge","the footbridge had never come up before these two speakers addressed it"],"a":2,"e":"The speakers are committed to opposite answers on the credited point, and not on the tempting one. The attractive choice — the footbridge both speakers mention was actually under discussion — loses because both speakers can accept it, or neither has taken a stand on it. Disagreement needs opposite commitments."},
{"t":"Point at issue","pattern":"Point at issue","d":3,"s":"Esme: Riley finished the dock course, because Riley has the green badge and finishing is enough to earn one. Farid: Finishing is enough to earn the badge, but inspectors who never take the course also get badges, and Riley is an inspector, so the badge does not show that Riley finished.","q":"The statements provide the most support for the claim that the two speakers disagree over whether","c":["The Riley both speakers mention was actually under discussion","Finishing the course is enough to earn a green badge","One of the two speakers has a personal stake in how the question about the Riley comes out","Riley's badge shows that Riley finished the dock course","Every dispute of this kind has to be resolved the way the speakers resolve the Riley"],"a":3,"e":"The speakers are committed to opposite answers on the credited point, and not on the tempting one. The attractive choice — The Riley both speakers mention was actually under discussion — loses because both speakers can accept it, or neither has taken a stand on it. Disagreement needs opposite commitments."},
{"t":"Point at issue","pattern":"Point at issue","d":3,"s":"Noor: The chef's remark establishes that the cables should be replaced. Omar: The remark is a pastry chef applying no cable standard, so it does not establish that.","q":"The statements provide the most support for the claim that the two speakers disagree over whether","c":["the chef's both speakers mention was actually under discussion","one of the two speakers has a personal stake in how the question about the chef's comes out","every dispute of this kind has to be resolved the way the speakers resolve the chef's","the chef's had never come up before these two speakers addressed it","the chef's remark establishes that the cables should be replaced"],"a":4,"e":"The speakers are committed to opposite answers on the credited point, and not on the tempting one. The attractive choice — the chef's both speakers mention was actually under discussion — loses because both speakers can accept it, or neither has taken a stand on it. Disagreement needs opposite commitments."},
{"t":"Point at issue","pattern":"Point at issue","d":3,"s":"Tessa: The full set is light enough for one person to carry, because each case is. Uri: Each case is light, but the four together exceed the limit, so the set is not light enough.","q":"The statements provide the most support for the claim that the two speakers disagree over whether","c":["the full set is light enough for one person to carry","the light both speakers mention was actually under discussion","one of the two speakers has a personal stake in how the question about the light comes out","every dispute of this kind has to be resolved the way the speakers resolve the light","the light had never come up before these two speakers addressed it"],"a":0,"e":"The speakers are committed to opposite answers on the credited point, and not on the tempting one. The attractive choice — the light both speakers mention was actually under discussion — loses because both speakers can accept it, or neither has taken a stand on it. Disagreement needs opposite commitments."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Post hoc","d":2,"s":"The chapel bell was oiled on Monday and rang late on Tuesday. The sexton concludes that the oiling caused the late ringing.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that the oiling caused the late ringing follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case.","Nothing that happened between the oiling and the late ringing, other than the oiling, accounts for the lateness.","Late ringing is easier to hear than an on-time ring.","Oiling a bell always makes that bell ring late.","The sexton has oiled bells in other chapels."],"a":1,"e":"The conclusion needs there to be no rival account of this gap. It does not need the much stronger claim that the factor works in every case no matter what else differs. The attractive choice — Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that the oiling caused the late ringing follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case. — loses because it would carry the conclusion if it were granted, but the argument does not need a claim that strong. Sufficient is not the same as necessary."}
]);
